  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...    \nLate this week is shaping up to be a more active period for monsoon \nthunderstorms across at least South-Central AZ. By Thursday, \nanticyclonic subsidence will begin to diminish as the subtropical \nhigh weakens overhead, and abundant low level moisture will remain \nin place through the rest of the week and linger into the weekend. \nLatest NBM guidance seems to favor Friday with the best chances for \nlower desert thunderstorms, which makes sense given the weaker \nsubsidence aloft and plenty of moisture and instability to support \nnumerous thunderstorms over the high terrain by the afternoon, \nsending outflows into the lower deserts and triggering additional \nthunderstorms by the evening. GFS bufr soundings indicate a good \nbalance between CAPE and DCAPE Thursday evening, with values between \n1000-1500 J/kg, which is typically a good indicator for severe \nweather potential over the Valley. However, CIN may be a bit \nstronger than preferred, and the development of thunderstorms that \ncan utilize this environment will be entirely dependent on the \npresence and timing of a sufficient lifting mechanism (i.e., \nthunderstorm outflow boundary). This conditional threat continues \nFriday, but with perhaps lower DCAPE and shallower inverted-V \nsoundings. Strong thunderstorm winds, dense blowing dust, locally \nheavy rainfall resulting in instances of flash flooding, and \nfrequent lightning will all be on the table for thunderstorm hazards \nlater this week.\n\nThe forecast for this weekend into early next week is much less \ncertain, however, a few more details are coming into focus tonight \ncompared to the guidance available in previous forecast updates. \nEnsembles are in better agreement that mean troughing will establish \nover the Northwest US, pushing the northern stream and likely a \nseries of upper lows and their attendant jet streaks as far south as \nthe Great Basin. WPC cluster analysis reveals discrepancies on how \nfar south the mean troughing will establish and how strongly \nnegative the midlevel height anomalies will become over the \nNorthwest US. The current consensus is for flow aloft to shift south \nto southwesterly as a result, meaning the more typical monsoonal \npattern which briefly sets up this week will be short-lived, \nbecoming something more akin to a transition season setup. This does \nnot, however, mean that thunderstorm chances leave the forecast. \nDrying will begin to occur from west to east, but low level moisture \nwill not be scoured out from Southern AZ. Thunderstorms will likely \ncontinue to favor development over prominent terrain features, and \nlower desert storm chances will be dependent on outflows or a nearly \nuncapped environment. As for now, NBM/WPC PoPs have come in lower \nthis weekend compared to Thu/Fri, and timing of the better chances \nseems a bit unrealistic, reflecting the high uncertainty and \nnebulous forcing mechanisms. Regardless, the main message for this \nweekend into early next week will be continued thunderstorm chances \nover at least South-Central AZ higher terrain areas, but likely a \ndecrease in activity from Thu/Fri due to the pattern shift and until \nsoutheasterly flow returns. \n\nWith the subtropical high diminished and then displaced by Northwest \nUS troughing this weekend, temperatures will follow a downward \ntrend, attaining near normal values likely by Saturday or Sunday, \nending the extreme heat conditions.",
